    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I'm a bit confused by the analogies. Could you stick to the physics for a second? How do those specific overtones physically manifest in the air column to differentiate a wooden flute from a metal one?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Certainly, let's leave the goblin antics aside and delve into the physics of it. When you play a flute, whether it's made of wood or metal, you're exciting the air column inside it. This excitement causes the air to vibrate, and these vibrations are what produce sound. The fundamental frequency is the lowest frequency of vibration and is what we perceive as the pitch of the note being played. However, the sound of the flute is also composed of overtones, which are higher frequencies that occur at integer multiples of the fundamental frequency.\n\nThe material of the flute affects the overtones because it influences the speed of sound within the air column and the way the air column resonates. In a wooden flute, the wood's density and elasticity cause the overtones to have a warmer, richer sound. The wood absorbs some of the higher frequencies, which can make the overtones less pronounced compared to a metal flute. On the other hand, a metal flute, being more rigid and less dense, allows the overtones to be more pronounced and brighter. The metal's ability to conduct sound efficiently means that the overtones are more distinct and can be heard more clearly.\n\nThe specific overtones that are produced depend on the shape and size of the flute, as well",
      "source": "target",
